# The l10n catalogues are key sets, not line sequences.
#
# Every branch that adds a translatable string writes to these four files, so a
# line-based merge put every branch in conflict with every other one. They are
# sorted now, which separates most additions, and what is left is merged key by
# key by tools/merge-l10n.js: a key one side added is kept, a key one side
# deleted is dropped, and only a key BOTH sides translated differently is a
# conflict.
#
# `merge=l10n` names the driver; the command behind the name is LOCAL config,
# because git refuses to run a command a repository ships. `npm install`
# registers it, and docs/Technical/l10n-merge-driver.md has the one line for a
# clone that already ran install. Without the registration git silently falls
# back to its ordinary merge, which is what happened before this file existed,
# so nothing breaks — it is just slower to resolve.
#
# `merge=union` would be wrong here and is deliberately not used: it
# concatenates both hunks, which on JSON drops a comma or duplicates a closing
# brace, and a catalogue that will not parse takes every string in the app down
# to its raw key.
l10n/en.json merge=l10n
l10n/nl.json merge=l10n

# The generated browser catalogues inherit their source's order, so they
# conflicted on the same line. The driver reads the OC.L10N.register shape too.
l10n/en.js merge=l10n
l10n/nl.js merge=l10n
